.TH GXWI-FEATURE-MANAGER 1 2026-10-19 "Peios gxwi-feature-manager 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
gxwi-feature-manager \- turn this machine's features on and off, on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-feature-manager
.SH DESCRIPTION
Feature Manager is the desktop's window on features: services and settings that set a machine up beyond the files packages install.
Each feature comes in a package, and one installed is listed here.
Each is a card: its title and name, what it says it does, and its state, which is Not Installed, Installed, Off, On, Interrupted, or Definition Missing when its package was removed while it was set up.
.PP
Under each card are the changes that apply to it.
Turn On installs a feature if it is not installed and turns it on; Install Only installs it without turning it on; Turn Off turns it off and leaves it installed; Remove... asks first, then turns it off if it is on and removes it, leaving its package installed.
A feature whose script did not finish is Interrupted: its card says which script, and Try Again runs it again.
.PP
It has no authority of its own.
It runs feat as the person, reading its list and following each change as feat makes it: while a change is made, the card shows which script is running and the last thing it said; if a script fails, the card shows everything it said.
Closing the window during a change does not stop it: the window stays, saying so, until the change is finished, and then closes.
.PP
Whether the person can change a feature is asked of the system, by feat: it needs write access to the feature's state in the registry, which as shipped only Administrators have.
Anyone else sees every feature and its state, with the reason said once at the top, and no buttons.
Being allowed to change a feature does not make every script able to do its work: a script that needs more than the person has fails, and the card says so.
.PP
It takes no arguments.
Without a desktop to open on, it says so and ends.
.SH SECURITY
Feature changes run through feat as the requester.
The backend enforces access and owns the operation's state.
Closing a window does not cancel an approved operation.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The application session ended normally.
.TP
.B 1
A connection, initialization, or application error prevented normal operation.
.TP
.B 64
Invalid command arguments.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GXWI_DISPLAY
The display connection supplied by the desktop session.
Launch through the desktop so inherited session descriptors and identity are available.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-feature-manager.toml
Its application declaration, listed in a launcher as Feature Manager.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/feat
The program it runs to list features and to change them.
.SH SEE ALSO
Feature Manager, Using feat, and Features, at learn.peios.org.
